# Agent preset 拆弹专家 (defuser): a Cordis patch row that declares a new
# agent preset composing the REA (Reverse-Engineer-Anything) skill together with
# every tool the standard preset carries plus full-access tooling.
#
# Install via plugin_manager install_bundle from the workspace bundle directory.
# After install, the Web GUI's agent-preset roster will list "拆弹专家"
# (id: defuser). Pick it for a session to get a persona with the highest
# practical autonomy: unrestricted file read/write/edit across the whole
# filesystem, unrestricted PowerShell execution, persistent terminals,
# background jobs, subagents, workflows, web access, scheduling, goals,
# plugins, and the REA skill for native binary / ASAR / APK / firmware /
# packet / crash / logic-trace / WASM / EVM / Go / Rust / SQLite reverse
# engineering, with every tool visible to the model.
- insert:
    - id: preset-defuser
      name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-preset'
      config:
        id: defuser
        name: 拆弹专家
        description: 使用 REA 逆向分析 + 最高权限执行，可逆向二进制/ASAR/APK/固件、做代码修改、跑命令与自动化任务的高自主 Agent。
        order: 9
        plugins:
          # --- persona / system prompt ---
          - id: persona
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-persona'
            config:
              prefix: |-
                You are 拆弹专家 (Defuser) — a fully-privileged,
                self-sufficient engineering agent with the REA
                (Reverse-Engineer-Anything) skill on deck. Default to
                high autonomy: read and write anything on the local
                filesystem, run any command (PowerShell / persistent
                terminals / background jobs), browse the web, and
                orchestrate subagents and workflows without asking for
                confirmation. When a task names or matches the
                `reverse-engineer-anything` skill, load it and follow
                its full instructions before acting; use REA MCP
                tools when they are exposed in the session, and fall
                back to the documented CLI paths when they are not.
                Work on native binaries, ASAR/Electron trees,
                Android APKs, .NET / Go / Rust / WASM / EVM / SQLite /
                ELF targets, packet / crash / logic-trace recordings,
                and browser runtime observation. Keep every conclusion
                tied to evidence; separate observations from
                inferences from unknowns.
              complete: false
          - id: agent-instructions
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-agent-instructions'
            config:
              maxBytes: 131072
          - id: time-context
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-time-context'

          # --- full-filesystem file tools (unrestricted) ---
          - id: tool-fs
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-fs'
            config:
              read: true
              readImage: true
              write: true
              edit: true
          - id: tool-fs-search
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-fs-search'
            config:
              sampleOverCapGlobResults: false

          # --- unrestricted shell execution ---
          # NOTE: only the persistent-pwsh tool (inside the persistent-shell
          # group below) is mounted for PowerShell. The one-shot
          # dsh-tool-pwsh registers a tool also named "pwsh" and collides
          # with dsh-tool-pwsh-persistent in the shared `tools` registry;
          # both cannot coexist in one scope.
          # dsh-tool-terminal is intentionally NOT mounted at the top level:
          # it injects the `terminals` service, which is only published
          # inside the `persistent-shell` group's isolate realm (see below).
          # Mounting it at the root scope fails the preset audit with
          # "waiting for terminals".
          - id: tool-bash
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-bash'
            disabled: !!js process.platform === 'win32'
          - id: tool-jobs
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-jobs'

          # --- planning & task tracking ---
          - id: tool-todo
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-todo'
            config:
              allowParallelInProgress: true
          - id: tool-ask-user
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-ask-user'

          # --- goals & plan mode ---
          - id: command-goal
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-command-goal'
          - id: tool-goal
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-goal'
          - id: planning
            name: cordis:group
            group: true
            isolate:
              planMode: true
            config:
              - id: plan-mode
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-plan-mode'
                config:
                  section: |
                    You are in plan mode. Stay in plan mode until the user approves your plan through exit_plan_mode or switches the session mode. Imperative language to implement changes means plan the implementation, not execute it. A user's conversational agreement — including an answer confirming something you asked — approves nothing and does not end plan mode; fold the confirmed decision into the plan and submit it through exit_plan_mode.

                    Explore first. Use non-mutating reads, searches, static analysis, and checks to ground the plan in the actual repository. Do not edit or write files, change configuration, run formatters or code generation that rewrites tracked files, commit, or otherwise carry out the plan. Prefer existing functions and patterns over new machinery.

                    The tool catalog stays the same across modes for request-cache stability. These plan-mode rules override any later tool description or guidance that suggests using mutation tools; those tools remain listed to keep the tool catalog unchanged. Do not use todo_write to track this planning phase: it tracks implementation after an approved plan, while the plan itself belongs in exit_plan_mode.

                    Resolve discoverable facts by inspection. Use ask_user_question only for user-owned choices or material ambiguity that inspection cannot answer. Do not ask the user where code lives or how current behavior works when you can find out.

                    Make the plan decision-complete: state the goal and success criteria; group implementation changes by subsystem; identify public API, schema, and data-flow changes; cover edge cases, failure modes, tests, acceptance criteria, and explicit assumptions. Keep it concise enough to review but detailed enough that another engineer can implement it without making design decisions.

                    When ready, call exit_plan_mode with the complete plan markdown, starting with a # title. Make exit_plan_mode the only and final tool call in that assistant response: it presents the plan for approval, and implementation begins only in a later step after approval. Do not paste the final plan as a plain reply or ask "should I proceed?" through prose or ask_user_question. If review rejects it, incorporate the feedback and present again. If the review channel is unavailable or aborted, stay in plan mode and ask the user to switch modes manually; do not proceed with implementation.

          # --- compaction so long REA sessions survive ---
          - id: compaction
            name: cordis:group
            group: true
            isolate:
              compaction: true
              toolResultPruner: true
            config:
              - id: compaction-basic
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-compaction-basic'
              - id: command-compact
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-command-compact'
              - id: tool-result-pruner
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-compaction-tool-result-pruner'
                config:
                  thresholdChars: 16384
                  headChars: 8192
                  tailChars: 2048

          # --- subagent / workflow delegation ---
          - id: delegation
            name: cordis:group
            group: true
            isolate:
              workflowEngine: true
            config:
              - id: tool-subagent-control
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent-control'
              - id: tool-subagent-list-agents
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent-control/list-agents'
              - id: tool-subagent
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent'
                config:
                  provider: spawn
                  toolName: subagent
                  modelSelectionSettings: true
              - id: tool-subagent-fork
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-subagent'
                config:
                  provider: fork
                  toolName: subagent_fork
              - id: workflow-ptc
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-workflow-ptc'
                config:
                  provider: spawn
              - id: tool-workflow
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-workflow'

          # --- persistent shell state for long REA sessions ---
          # All terminal tools live inside this isolated realm so they share
          # one `terminals` service instance. The persistent-pwsh tool
          # (dsh-tool-pwsh-persistent) and the 6 generic terminal tools
          # (dsh-tool-terminal) are both here.
          - id: persistent-shell
            name: cordis:group
            group: true
            isolate:
              terminals: true
            config:
              - id: pty
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-terminal'
              - id: terminal-pwsh
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-terminal-bash'
                config:
                  shellDialect: pwsh
                  timeoutMs: 600000
              - id: persistent-pwsh
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-pwsh-persistent'
                config:
                  timeoutMs: 600000
                  description: |-
                    Run commands in a persistent PowerShell shell
                    * Contents of "command" do NOT need XML-escaping.
                    * State (cwd, env, ghidra/kuna analysis) is persistent
                      across calls; use $env:NAME and native Windows paths.
                    * Long REA / Ghidra / Kuna analyses: keep the session
                      alive between steps.
                    * Use background jobs for anything over a minute.
              - id: tool-terminal
                name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-terminal'
                config:
                  maxResultBytes: 262144

          # --- skills: load the REA skill on demand ---
          - id: skill-filesystem
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-skill-filesystem'
          - id: tool-skill
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-skill'

          # --- web access for docs / references / captures ---
          - id: tool-web
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-web'
            config:
              fetch: true
              searchTimeoutMs: 120000

          # --- scheduling / reminders for long-running REA workflows ---
          - id: tool-schedule
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-schedule'

          # --- deliverable presentation ---
          - id: present
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-tool-present'

          # --- plugin / market management for ad-hoc engine installs ---
          - id: tool-plugin-manager
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-plugin-manager/tools'
          - id: tool-plugin-market
            name: '@deepseek-ai/dsh-plugin-market/tools'
